                <pb facs="0958" n="700" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg700"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 851.731/10</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Sharp</hi>)
                        <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-01-16">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">January 16, 1915</hi>.</date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 8.30 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>464. Referring to Department’s instructions 349, November 20, and its telegram
                    369, December 11.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d981-f1"/> Foreign Office just advises me
                    that Bentley, Broomhall, and Meyer codes have been admitted for telegraph
                    messages sent over French lines under same conditions presented for codes
                    previously admitted, which was reported in Embassy’s 299 of November 21
                        [22].<anchor n="2" xml:id="d981-f2"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sharp</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
